    LICENSES/LicenseRef-Cygames-Game-Assets.txt.

While upstream's lines remain, the program as a whole can be distributed under
GPL-2.0 only.


Why upstream's code is GPL-2.0-only
-----------------------------------

Upstream's repository carries the GPL-2.0 text, added in a commit titled
"添加 GPL 2.0 许可证" (add the GPL 2.0 licence), and says nothing about later
versions. Section 9 of GPL-2.0 allows any version only when the program "does
not specify a version number". Whether shipping the version 2 text under that
title specifies one is open to argument. Only the narrower reading cannot turn
out to be wrong, so upstream's code is treated as GPL-2.0-only.

LowGuiGui's own work is offered under GPL-2.0-or-later. That keeps a GPLv3
route open for when upstream's lines are gone, or upstream agrees to "or
later". The route matters because OpenCV is licensed under Apache-2.0, which
the FSF considers compatible with GPLv3 but not with GPLv2.


Modified files
--------------

GPL-2.0 section 2(a) asks that modified files carry prominent notices saying
that they were changed, and when. The headers of opencv.py, pagetree.py and
.gitignore say that they derive from upstream and since when LowGuiGui has
changed them. The git history records each change and its date, and
docs/provenance/ maps the commits made before this repository existed back to
the archive.


About the AI-written code
-------------------------

The fork's changes and this repository's code were written by an AI coding
assistant (Anthropic's Claude Code) at the owner's direction, and were not
reviewed line by line by a human. Whether AI-generated output is protected by
copyright is unsettled; to the extent it is, the notices above apply. The
README says more, including what has been verified and how.
